"Behind the pupil is the lens, which fine-tunes the focus. A ring of tiny muscles around it changes its shape. To see something far away, the lens is pulled flatter. To see something up close, the muscles tighten and the lens gets rounder, so it bends light more. Light from each point of an object comes together at one point on the retina, the thin layer at the back of the eye. But here is a surprise: the picture on your retina is upside down and flipped left to right. Your brain sorts this out, so the world looks right side up.": "5f1bf8d3ca3b",
