"Some eyes have trouble focusing. In a nearsighted eye, the eyeball is a little too long. Light from faraway things comes to a point in front of the retina, so the board at school looks blurry, but a book up close looks sharp. In a farsighted eye, the eyeball is a little too short, so things up close are harder to see clearly. Glasses fix both problems by bending the light a little before it enters the eye. Lenses for nearsighted eyes are thinner in the middle and spread the light out. Lenses for farsighted eyes are thicker in the middle and bring the light together.": "e8306ad7ebb2",
